course: biology
lesson: 146
title: Shrek's dragon: real «dragons» and lizards, and why nothing breathes fire
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: In Shrek, a dragon as big as a castle breathes great flames, flies and guards a castle full of treasure. No real animal does any of that.
explain: Breathing fire would need a store of fuel and a spark, and the animal's own soft, wet mouth would burn; the bombardier beetle comes closest, firing a nearly boiling chemical spray from a tough chamber.
explain: Flying gets harder the heavier you are, because wings and muscles must grow even faster than the body; the biggest fliers ever, pterosaurs with 10-metre wings, were light with hollow bones, so a castle-sized dragon could never take off.
explain: Yet real «dragons» exist: the Komodo dragon flicking its forked yellow tongue on an Indonesian beach, the tiny flying dragon gliding between trees, and the bearded dragon basking on a warm rock in a pet shop tank.
fact: No real animal breathes fire; the bombardier beetle sprays a nearly boiling chemical mix from a tough chamber at attackers.
fact: The Komodo dragon is the biggest lizard, up to about 3 metres long and around 70 kilograms, and lives on a few Indonesian islands with no big cats or wolves.
fact: Komodo dragons have venom glands in their jaws; the venom stops blood clotting and weakens prey.
fact: A Komodo dragon smells with its forked tongue, which carries scent to an organ in the roof of its mouth, its two tips telling left from right, and can find a dead animal kilometres away.
fact: A Komodo dragon can eat up to about 80 percent of its own weight in one meal and then wait weeks.
fact: Female Komodo dragons in British zoos laid eggs that hatched with no father, a rare virgin birth called parthenogenesis.
fact: Draco lizards of South-East Asia, called flying dragons, glide over 10 metres on flaps of skin held out on long ribs.
fact: The Australian frilled lizard opens a huge neck frill and hisses to startle predators, and if that fails it runs off on its back legs.
fact: Many lizards drop their tails when grabbed: the tail breaks at weak points and muscles close the blood vessels, so little blood is lost; the tail wriggles to keep the predator busy, and the new tail grows with a rod of cartilage instead of bone.
fact: The largest fliers ever, pterosaurs like Quetzalcoatlus, had wings about 10 metres across but were light, with hollow bones.
fact: No animal collects gold; bowerbirds decorate their display areas with blue or shiny objects, and the idea that magpies steal shiny things is mostly a myth, as tests found magpies are wary of shiny objects.
fact: Bearded dragons, like all reptiles, warm up on sun or warm rock before they can move fast, and wave one arm slowly to show they give in.
fact: Chinese water dragons live beside rivers, jump into water when scared and can stay under for around 25 minutes.
exercise: Why can no real animal breathe fire? | It would need fuel and a spark, and its soft mouth would burn | Animals are not allowed to breathe fire by law | Fire needs too much water to make | All the fire-breathing animals died out a long time ago, so none are left
exercise: Which real animal comes closest to breathing fire? | The bombardier beetle, with its nearly boiling spray | The Komodo dragon, with its hot breath | The frilled lizard, which shoots out fire from behind its frill | The firefly, with its burning light
exercise: What is the Komodo dragon? | The biggest lizard in the world | A small kind of crocodile | A dinosaur that is still alive | A snake with four legs
exercise: Where do Komodo dragons live in the wild? | On a few islands of Indonesia | All over the deserts of Africa | In the rivers of South America | In the misty forests of southern China
exercise: What does a Komodo dragon's forked tongue do? | It smells, telling left from right | It tastes the food after biting it, to check whether it is good to eat | It cools the lizard down | It catches flies like a frog
exercise: What does Komodo venom do to prey? | Stops blood clotting and weakens the prey | Turns the prey slowly to stone, like in old tales | Makes the prey fall asleep at once, so it does not feel any pain | Has no effect at all; Komodos have no venom
exercise: What is the «flying dragon» of South-East Asia? | A Draco lizard that glides on skin flaps held out on ribs | A bat with a long scaly tail | A bird that looks like a lizard | A lizard that flaps feathered wings, like a small bird or a bat
exercise: What does a frilled lizard do when scared? | Opens a huge neck frill and hisses | Breathes out a puff of smoke | Changes into a green leaf | Digs into the sand at once
exercise: Why does a lizard's dropped tail keep wriggling? | To keep the predator busy while the lizard escapes | Because it is trying to crawl back | Because it is still looking for food | Because it wants to grow back into a whole new lizard on its own
exercise: Why could a castle-sized dragon never fly? | Beyond a certain weight, no real muscles could lift it | Its wings would be too light | The air is too thin near the tops of castles and mountains for wings | Big animals are scared of heights
exercise: How big were the largest flying animals ever? | Wings about 10 metres across, with light hollow bones | Wings about 100 metres across, with heavy bones | Bigger than a castle, with stone bones | About the size of an eagle, no bigger, because big wings are too heavy
exercise: Does any real animal hoard gold like a dragon? | No, but bowerbirds decorate with shiny or blue things | Yes, magpies fill their nests with gold rings stolen from people's homes | Yes, Komodo dragons guard piles of gold in their caves | Yes, crows bury gold coins in the ground to save for winter
exercise: How did zoo Komodo dragons hatch young with no father? | By a rare virgin birth called parthenogenesis | The eggs were laid by a male | The keepers added a father later | The eggs were really crocodile eggs that had been put in by mistake
exercise: Why does a bearded dragon lie flat on a warm rock? | It warms up so it can move fast | It is hiding from the sun | It is cooling down on the rock | It is sleeping until winter
exercise: What does a scared Chinese water dragon do? | Drops into the water and hides under it | Climbs quickly to the very top of the tree to get away | Breathes out little bubbles of fire at the enemy | Rolls up in a tight ball like a hedgehog
exercise: How much can a Komodo dragon eat in one meal? | Up to about 80 percent of its weight | About one percent of its weight | Twice its own weight | Only one mouse at a time
test: «3 lizards in the tank, one dropped its tail yesterday, how many tails still attached» | 2 | 3 | 1 | 4
test: «dragons in Shrek breathe fire so some real lizard probably does too, right?» What is right? | No real animal breathes fire; its own mouth would burn | Yes, the Komodo dragon breathes small flames when it is hunting | Yes, the bearded dragon puffs out smoke | Yes, but only at night when it is cold
test: In a cartoon a lizard loses its tail and screams in agony with blood everywhere. What is real? | The tail breaks at weak points and muscles close the vessels, so little blood is lost | Losing a tail always kills a lizard | The tail can never grow back | The lizard picks the tail up later and sticks it back on with spit
test: What would happen if a bearded dragon were kept in a cold tank with no warm rock? | It would be slow and sluggish, because reptiles warm up from outside | It would make its own heat and be fine | It would grow thick fur to stay warm, like a bear in the snow | It would move faster to keep warm
test: «magpie nicked my mum's ring, they're shiny thieves» What is right? | Tests found magpies are wary of shiny objects; the story is mostly a myth | Yes, magpies steal every shiny thing they see, like rings and keys and spoons | Yes, magpies build gold nests | No, magpies collect only blue things
test: A Komodo dragon on an island with no wolves or big cats. Why did it become the top hunter? | No bigger meat-eaters lived there to compete with it | It is the fastest animal on Earth | Its fire breath scared off all rivals | It ate all the wolves long ago
test: «a lizard's tongue split in 2 at the end, how many tips have 4 Komodos got between them» | 8 | 4 | 2 | 16
test: Why might parthenogenesis help a lone female Komodo that reaches an empty island? | She could start a new group without a male | She could fly back to her old island to find a husband and lay eggs there | She would never need to eat again, living off the sun | She could turn into a male later and find a female
test: «crap, the beardie's waving one arm at me, is it angry» What is it most likely saying? | It is signalling that it gives in | It is about to attack whoever is near | It wants to shake hands like a person | It is waving goodbye before running
test: A Draco lizard leaps from one tree trunk and lands on another 10 metres away. How? | It glides on skin flaps spread out on its long ribs | It flaps feathered wings like a bird | It jumps with very strong back legs | The wind blows it across like a leaf
test: «Komodo just bites and lets go, why'd it bother» Why is one bite often enough? | Its venom stops blood clotting and weakens the prey | It is just tasting the animal | It is too tired to hold on | Its teeth fall out after one bite, so it has to wait for new ones to grow
exam: Identify the principal reason for which no living animal is capable of breathing fire. | The production of flame would require stored fuel and ignition, and the soft tissues of the mouth would be destroyed | Such capacity is prohibited by the laws of the countries concerned | The production of flame requires a quantity of water no animal can store, and the throat would be extinguished | All such animals became extinct within recent memory
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the size of the largest flying animals known. | Certain pterosaurs attained a wingspan of approximately ten metres, their bones being hollow and light | Certain dragons attained the dimensions of a castle | No flying animal has exceeded the dimensions of a pigeon | Certain pterosaurs attained a wingspan of one hundred metres, their bones being solid and exceedingly heavy
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the reproduction observed in female Komodo dragons in British zoos? | Eggs were laid and hatched in the absence of any male, by parthenogenesis | Eggs were laid by a male and incubated by a female | No eggs were produced in the absence of a male | The eggs were subsequently found to be those of crocodiles, introduced into the enclosure in error
exam: Ascertain the function of the forked tongue of the Komodo dragon. | It gathers scent for an organ in the roof of the mouth and distinguishes direction | It serves principally for the tasting of food already bitten, and for testing whether it is safe to eat | It serves for the lowering of the body temperature | It serves for the capture of flying insects
